## Proof

Let $G = \text{Gal}(E/F)$. By the fundamental theorem of Galois theory, for any subgroup $K \leq G$ with fixed field $E_K$:

- $E/E_K$ is Galois with $\text{Gal}(E/E_K) \cong K$, and $[E : E_K] = |K|$;
- $[E_K : F] = [G : K]$.

Apply this to $K = H$ and $K = N(H)$ (the normalizer of $H$ in $G$). Since $H \leq N(H)$, we have $E_{N(H)} \subseteq E_H$, so the tower

$$E \supseteq E_H \supseteq E_{N(H)} \supseteq F$$

with
$$[E : E_H] = |H|,\qquad [E : E_{N(H)}] = |N(H)|,\qquad [E_{N(H)}:F] = [G:N(H)].$$

**Galois-ness of $E_H/E_{N(H)}$.** The extension $E/E_{N(H)}$ is Galois with group $N(H)$. Under the Galois correspondence, the intermediate field $E_H$ corresponds to the subgroup $H \leq N(H)$. The extension $E_H/E_{N(H)}$ is Galois if and only if $H \trianglelefteq N(H)$. But this is exactly what the normalizer guarantees:

$$N(H) = \{g \in G : gHg^{-1} = H\},$$

so $H$ is normal in $N(H)$ by construction. Hence $E_H/E_{N(H)}$ is indeed Galois, with

$$\text{Gal}(E_H/E_{N(H)}) \cong N(H)/H.$$

**Computing the degree.** Therefore

$$[E_H : E_{N(H)}] = |N(H)/H| = \frac{|N(H)|}{|H|}.$$

To express this using the index $[G:N(H)]$: since $|G| = [G:N(H)]\cdot|N(H)|$, we have $|N(H)| = |G|/[G:N(H)]$, and also $[E_H:F] = [G:H] = |G|/|H|$ and $[E_{N(H)}:F] = [G:N(H)]$. Using multiplicativity of degrees in the tower $E_H \supseteq E_{N(H)} \supseteq F$,

$$[E_H : F] = [E_H : E_{N(H)}]\cdot [E_{N(H)} : F],$$

$$\frac{|G|}{|H|} = [E_H : E_{N(H)}]\cdot [G:N(H)].$$

Solving:

$$\boxed{[E_H : E_{N(H)}] = \frac{[G:H]}{[G:N(H)]} = \frac{|N(H)|}{|H|} = \frac{|G|}{|H|\cdot [G:N(H)]}.}$$

Equivalently, the degree equals the index of $H$ in its normalizer:

$$[E_H : E_{N(H)}] = [N(H) : H] = \frac{|N(H)|}{|H|}.$$

